# Figma usage

## What Figma is authoritative for

Figma is the source of truth for visual fidelity:

- Layout, copy, color and spacing tokens
- Typography (family, size, weight, line height)
- Responsive intent inferred from frame structure and constraints
- Component states (hover, focus, active, disabled, etc.)

Honor the design pixel-faithfully where reasonable. When the design and the
existing token system disagree on a value, prefer the existing token and flag
the discrepancy.

## What Figma is NOT authoritative for

Figma's layer/frame/component tree is a designer-readability aid, not a code
architecture spec. Do NOT mirror it 1:1 into Astro components.

Decide component boundaries based on Astro conventions and existing patterns
in this repo. Default to inlining; split a piece into its own component only
when it has at least one of:

- genuine reuse across pages,
- non-trivial interactivity (client-side JS, state),
- shared logic worth isolating, or
- enough size that inlining hurts readability.

If a Figma frame is one-off, hardcoded, and has no logic, inline it.

When unsure whether to split, ask before creating the file.

## Heuristic

> Would I make this its own component if Figma didn't have it as a frame?
> If no, don't.
